package view

// The bodies of the docs pages. Registered, in order, in docs.go.
//
// Every claim here is about what is deployed. If the code changes, change the
// page; if a feature is not built, it appears only under a "Coming next" badge.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- getting started

templ docGettingStarted() {
	<p class="docs-lede">
		From nothing to a team, your assistants connected, and your board open in a browser. It takes one
		command and a couple of answers.
	</p>
	<h2 id="install">Install</h2>
	<p>Run this in a terminal on each machine you code on:</p>
	@docCode("once per machine", docInstall)
	<p>
		The script is served as plain text at that address, so you can read it before you run it. It is POSIX
		<code>sh</code> and never uses <code>sudo</code>. To see every change and every call it would make
		without doing any of them, run it with <code>--dry-run</code>.
	</p>
	<h2 id="create-or-join">Create a team, or join one</h2>
	<p>The installer asks which of two things you are doing:</p>
	<ul>
		<li><b>join</b>, when a teammate sent you a join code. You paste the code at the prompt, and it is not echoed.</li>
		<li><b>create</b>, when nobody has set the team up yet. You type a team name.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Creating a team prints its first join code once, in a box. Anyone holding that code can join the team
		and see its board, and that first code has no use limit and no expiry. So for each new teammate, send
		a fresh invite instead: see <a href="/docs/inviting-teammates">Inviting teammates</a>.
	</p>
	<p>Without a terminal to prompt in, set one environment variable instead. Never pass a secret as an argument:</p>
	@docCode("join a team", docInstallJoin)
	@docCode("create a team", docInstallMake)
	<p>
		The installer joins over the network before it writes anything. It then checks each assistant&#39;s new
		token on a fresh connection. If a check fails, nothing is written for that assistant, and re-running
		the installer is safe.
	</p>
	<h2 id="assistants">Which assistants get configured</h2>
	<p>
		The installer configures the assistants it finds on the machine, each as its own agent with its own token.
		Nothing is written inside your repository.
	</p>
	<div class="docs-table">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th>Assistant</th><th>What the installer does</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>Claude Code</td><td>Registers the server at user scope with <code>claude mcp add</code>, carrying Claude Code&#39;s own token, and installs the metiche-teamwork skill as a plugin.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Cursor</td><td>Adds a <code>metiche</code> entry to the global <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code>. It never writes the project-level file, which lives in your repository.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Windsurf</td><td>Adds a <code>metiche</code> entry to <code>~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json</code>.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Codex</td><td>Adds a <code>[mcp_servers.metiche]</code> table to <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code> and a delimited metiche block to the <code>AGENTS.md</code> beside it.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p>
		<code>--only claude,cursor</code> limits it to a subset. Other MCP clients, such as Zed, are not
		configured. Every existing file it changes is backed up first, next to itself.
	</p>
	<p><b>When it finishes, restart your assistants.</b> Each one is already authenticated as its own agent.</p>
	<h2 id="open-the-board">Open your board, signed in</h2>
	<p>
		The last step asks <code>Open your board in a browser now, signed in? [Y/n]</code>. On yes, it gets a
		sign-in link that signs one browser in as you, works once and expires after 10 minutes.
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>On your own desktop, the link goes into a private file that is opened and then removed, never onto a command line.</li>
		<li>Over SSH, the link is printed so you can open it on your own machine.</li>
		<li>It only asks in a terminal, and never when <code>CI</code> is set. In those cases no link is created at all.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Later, ask your assistant to <b>open the metiche board</b>. It calls <code>open_board</code> and gives you a
		fresh link. If you are on one team the link opens that team&#39;s board; if you are on several it opens your
		list of teams. <a href="/docs/the-board#signing-in">More on signing in</a>.
	</p>
	<div class="docs-note soon" id="command-line">
		<span class="h">Command line (coming soon)</span>
		<p>
			A metiche command line is being built. It is not available yet, so these docs do not describe it.
			When it ships, it gets its own page here. Until then, everything above works through the installer
			and your assistant.
		</p>
	</div>
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- inviting

templ docInviting() {
	<p class="docs-lede">
		A join code lets someone new onto your team. Anyone on the team can make one, from the board or by asking
		an assistant.
	</p>
	<h2 id="board">From the board</h2>
	<p>
		Open your team&#39;s board signed in and choose the <b>Invites</b> tab. It is shown only to a signed-in member
		of the team, and never on a demo board. The page has a form for a label, a number of uses and an expiry,
		set to the defaults and limited to what your role allows. It also lists the team&#39;s invites with their
		uses, expiry and state.
	</p>
	<p>
		After you create one, the code is shown once, in a panel with a copy button. Copy it then: reloading the
		page does not show it again, and the board keeps no copy.
	</p>
	<h2 id="assistant">By asking your assistant</h2>
	<p>
		Say something like <b>make a metiche invite for Ana</b>. The assistant calls <code>create_invite</code>,
		optionally with a label, <code>max_uses</code> and <code>expires_in_hours</code>, and shows you the code.
	</p>
	<h2 id="limits">Defaults and limits</h2>
	<div class="docs-table">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th></th><th>Uses</th><th>Expires after</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>Default</td><td>1</td><td>7 days</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Most a team owner can set</td><td>100</td><td>30 days</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Most a member can set</td><td>25</td><td>7 days</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p>
		The board and the assistant tools run the same rules. Each account can create 30 invites an hour, and that
		one budget is shared between both.
	</p>
	<h2 id="shown-once">The code is shown once</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>It appears only in the response that created it: the board&#39;s one-time panel, or the assistant&#39;s <code>create_invite</code> answer.</li>
		<li><code>list_invites</code> and the Invites list never show codes.</li>
		<li>Retrying a <code>create_invite</code> with the same <code>idempotency_key</code> returns the same invite without its code.</li>
		<li>Lost it? Revoke that invite and make a new one.</li>
	</ul>
	<h2 id="revoke">Revoking</h2>
	<p>
		Use the <b>Revoke</b> button on the Invites tab, or ask your assistant, which calls <code>revoke_invite</code> with
		the invite&#39;s <code>invite_id</code>. Nobody new can join with that code afterwards. People who already joined
		with it keep their access, and revoking an invite twice changes nothing.
	</p>
	<p>
		A team&#39;s owner sees and can revoke every invite. A member sees and can revoke only the invites they created.
		An invite is <code>active</code>, <code>expired</code>, <code>exhausted</code> (its uses are spent) or
		<code>revoked</code>.
	</p>
	<h2 id="teammate-joins">How the teammate joins</h2>
	<p>Send the code privately, the way you would send a password. Your teammate runs the installer, chooses <b>join</b> and pastes it:</p>
	@docCode("on your teammate's machine", docInstall)
	<p>
		The prompt does not echo the code. Without a terminal, they set <code>METICHE_JOIN_CODE</code> instead,
		as shown on <a href="/docs/getting-started#create-or-join">Getting started</a>. Their installer configures
		their own assistants, each with its own token.
	</p>
	<div class="docs-note warn">
		<span class="h">A join code is a door code, not a token</span>
		<p>
			It is redeemed once, by the installer. What your assistants keep is a token the server mints for each
			of them. Never put a join code in a repository, a commit, an issue, a pull request or a public channel.
		</p>
	</div>
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the board

templ docBoard() {
	<p class="docs-lede">
		The board is the page where a person sees the whole team at once. Your team&#39;s board is at
		<code>metiche.xyz/t/&lt;team slug&gt;</code>. It updates live as events arrive, so there is nothing to refresh.
	</p>
	<p>
		Every board is read-only. Nothing on it resolves, dismisses or nudges anything; agents do that through
		their own tools. The one exception is the Invites tab, for signed-in members.
	</p>
	<h2 id="lanes">Lanes</h2>
	<p>
		Each member of the team gets a lane, with their agents underneath. Every live session is its own card, so
		two terminals of one assistant show as two cards, told apart by their session keys. An agent with nothing
		live keeps its most recent finished session on the board. A card shows:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>the status line, which is what the agent says it is doing right now, and the session&#39;s goal;</li>
		<li>the current intent and its status;</li>
		<li>the paths it has claimed (four per claim, then a count of the rest);</li>
		<li>a badge for each open conflict, linking to the Conflicts tab;</li>
		<li>its session key, linking to that session&#39;s run.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>When a conflict is open, a strip across the top of the board names the worst one and what to do about it.</p>
	<h3 id="subagents">Subagents</h3>
	<p>
		When an agent delegates work and each subagent starts its own session with its supervisor&#39;s session key
		(see <a href="/docs/working-with-agents#subagents">Working with agents</a>), the subagent&#39;s card sits under its
		supervisor&#39;s and reads <b>↳ subagent of S-12</b>. The supervisor&#39;s card counts its subagents.
	</p>
	<h2 id="conflicts">Conflicts</h2>
	<p>
		Today metiche detects one kind of conflict: two sessions holding overlapping paths, where at least one of them
		is writing. Two reads never conflict. The Conflicts tab lists each open one with its severity, the paths,
		who is involved, how long it has been open and a suggested action.
	</p>
	<h3 id="settling">How agents settle a conflict</h3>
	<p>
		Nothing is blocked by a conflict; it is information. Agents settle it between themselves, usually by
		splitting the file or by one going first, and ask their person only when they can&#39;t. Then they release what
		they agreed to release: they drop the path, mark the intent done, or end the session.
	</p>
	<p>
		metiche closes a conflict by itself once no two sessions in it still hold overlapping live paths. While
		they still overlap, it stays open, whatever anybody wrote in a note. A claim that expired, or a session
		abandoned after it stopped heartbeating, also clears an overlap.
	</p>
	<h3 id="settled">Conflict history</h3>
	<p>
		Below the open conflicts, <b>History</b> lists every conflict that is over (resolved, dismissed or expired), newest
		first, 50 at a time with <b>Load older conflicts</b>. Filter it by status and by kind. Each row shows the
		conflict&#39;s key, kind and severity, the paths, the sessions in it (each linking to its run), when it was first
		detected and when it ended, and:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>how it was resolved: <code>coordinated</code> (an agent answered the notice with what they agreed), <code>split</code> (one side moved to other files and kept working), <code>yielded</code> (one side let go) or <code>superseded</code> (every session in it had ended);</li>
		<li>
			a note that says who released what, and when, and quotes what the agents reported. It starts with
			<b>Settled by the agents</b> when an agent&#39;s call released the path, or <b>Cleared by metiche</b> when a
			claim expired or a session was abandoned. Anything in a quoted note that looks like a credential is masked.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>A demo board lists the conflicts its recording settled under <b>Settled</b> instead, with no filters and no older pages.</p>
	<h2 id="runs">Runs</h2>
	<p>
		The Runs tab has one row per session: the live ones first, then every run the team has had, newest first, 50 at
		a time with <b>Load older runs</b>. A demo board shows only the sessions in its recording. Each row shows the session
		key, status, outcome, whether it was a subagent or had subagents, the agent, the project and when it ran.
	</p>
	<h3 id="run-detail">Run detail</h3>
	<p>Open a run to see the whole story of that session:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>its status, outcome, project, branch, last status line and closing note, and when it started and ended;</li>
		<li>a link to its supervisor if it was a subagent, and the subagents it delegated to;</li>
		<li>the intents it declared, the paths it claimed, and when each was released or expires;</li>
		<li>the conflicts it took part in, including how the settled ones were settled;</li>
		<li>its event log.</li>
	</ul>
	<h2 id="activity">Timeline and Activity</h2>
	<p>
		The timeline beside the board shows the latest events as they happen. <b>Load older events</b> at its top adds
		the events before them, 100 at a time. The <b>Activity</b> tab is the team&#39;s whole event log, newest first, 100
		at a time. Choose a kind to see only those events, or <b>only this run</b> on a row to see one session&#39;s. Each
		row shows what happened, the kind, who did it, the session (linking to its run) and when. A row is only the line
		an event records, never what a tool was sent or answered.
	</p>
	<p>A demo board&#39;s timeline has no older events to load, and its Activity tab shows what its recording has replayed so far.</p>
	<h2 id="graph">Graph</h2>
	<p>
		The Graph tab starts <b>Live</b>: the live sessions on the left, the areas of the repository they hold paths in on
		the right, and a crossing wherever two sessions hold the same area. Choose <b>Last 24 hours</b> or
		<b>Last 7 days</b> to draw that time range instead, with every session active in it and every area each one
		held paths in.
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Two sessions cross only where they held the same area at the same moment, from when each hold began and ended.</li>
		<li>An area two sessions held one after the other is drawn dashed, and is not a crossing.</li>
		<li>A ring marks an area a conflict was raised about, and the time range&#39;s conflicts are listed under the drawing.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>A demo board shows only the live graph. Contracts and Decisions have no history to show.</p>
	<h2 id="signing-in">Signing in and out</h2>
	<p>
		Every board has <b>Sign in</b> at the top right. There is no password. You sign in with a link from one of
		your agents: ask your assistant to <b>open the metiche board</b>, or say yes at the end of the installer. The
		link signs one browser in, works once and expires after 10 minutes.
	</p>
	<p>
		Signed in, the top right reads <b>signed in as</b> your name, which links to your account page, next to a
		<b>Sign out</b> button. A browser session lasts at most 30 days, and ends sooner if the browser goes
		unused for 7 days.
	</p>
	<h2 id="account">Your account page</h2>
	<p>At <code>/account</code>, signed in, you see:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>every browser signed in to your account, with a hint of which browser it is, when it signed in and when it was last seen;</li>
		<li>a <b>Revoke</b> button for each one except the browser you are using, which <b>Sign out</b> covers;</li>
		<li><b>Sign out everywhere</b>, which ends every signed-in browser, this one included;</li>
		<li><b>Recently signed out</b>: browsers that are no longer signed in, and why, kept for about a week.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Retiring the agent that opened a browser also signs that browser out. You can also ask your assistant: it
		calls <code>sign_out_browsers</code>, which only lists your browsers unless you ask it to sign one, or all,
		of them out. Opened without signing in, <code>/account</code> sends you to the sign-in page.
	</p>
	<h2 id="coming-next">Other tabs</h2>
	<p>
		@soon()
		The <b>Contracts</b> and <b>Decisions</b> tabs are there for features that are not built yet. They stay
		empty until then.
	</p>
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- repos and teams

templ docReposTeams() {
	<p class="docs-lede">
		A team&#39;s board is visible to every member of that team. So metiche never puts a repository&#39;s work there
		on a guess.
	</p>
	<h2 id="projects">A repository is a project</h2>
	<p>
		Claims, and so conflicts, are scoped to a project, and a project is a repository. Your agent names it from git:
		the <code>origin</code> remote, the git root folder&#39;s name and the current branch. metiche matches the
		project by the remote first, so every agent in one repository lands on the same project even if they spell the
		name differently. A project name that already belongs to a different repository on the team is refused rather
		than merged.
	</p>
	<h2 id="the-question">The one-time question</h2>
	<p>
		The first time an agent starts work in a repository that is not yet a project on the team, metiche creates
		nothing. It answers with <code>confirm_repo_binding</code>, and your agent asks you, word for word:
	</p>
	@docCode("", docBindingQ)
	<ul>
		<li><b>Yes:</b> the agent starts again with your confirmation and writes a <code>.metiche</code> file so nobody is asked again.</li>
		<li><b>No:</b> the agent leaves metiche out of this repository, or creates a separate team for this work.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Once the repository is a project on the team, nobody on that team is asked about it again. An agent must only
		confirm when you actually said yes.
	</p>
	<h2 id="metiche-file">The .metiche file</h2>
	<p>Two lines, in a file named <code>.metiche</code> at the git root:</p>
	@docCode(".metiche", docMeticheFile)
	<ul>
		<li><code>team</code> is the team&#39;s slug and <code>project</code> is the project&#39;s key.</li>
		<li>
			Agents look for it by walking up from their working directory to the git root. A file found there names the
			team, so the agent can start a new project without asking you. It still has to be a member of that team.
		</li>
		<li>Commit it, and your teammates&#39; agents are not asked either. It names a team and a project and nothing else: never put a token, a join code or a link in it.</li>
	</ul>
	<h2 id="several-teams">Working with several teams</h2>
	<p>
		One identity can be on several teams: join another team with a new join code and you keep the same
		account. On more than one team:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>A call that needs a team and does not say which is refused with the list of your team slugs. A <code>.metiche</code> file, or you, decides.</li>
		<li>An agent with no <code>.metiche</code> file asks you which team a repository belongs to, and then writes the file. With exactly one team it uses that team and tells you once.</li>
		<li>Asking an assistant to open the board without naming a team opens your list of teams. Signed in, <code>/teams</code> shows <b>Your teams</b>.</li>
		<li>When you re-run the installer, <code>METICHE_TEAM_SLUG</code> says which team to use.</li>
	</ul>
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- agents

templ docAgents() {
	<p class="docs-lede">
		Your agent coordinates on its own. The metiche-teamwork skill, which the installer sets up where it can,
		teaches it the loop. This page is what that looks like from your side.
	</p>
	<h2 id="loop">What the agent does on its own</h2>
	@docCode("the loop", docLoop)
	<ol class="docs-steps">
		<li><b><code>start_session</code></b> once per piece of work. It names the repository from git and says what the session is for. The answer is a <code>session_key</code> to send on every later call.</li>
		<li><b><code>declare_intent</code></b> before it edits anything: one sentence of up to 280 characters, the files and the mode. This creates the claim, and any conflict comes back in the same response.</li>
		<li><b><code>heartbeat</code></b> about every 60 seconds while it works, with a status line of up to 120 characters. It keeps the session alive and extends its claims. <b><code>update_intent</code></b> changes the status line, adds or drops paths, or marks the intent done. Added paths are checked like a declaration.</li>
		<li><b><code>end_session</code></b> when it is done, with an outcome: succeeded, failed or abandoned. The session&#39;s claims are released at once.</li>
	</ol>
	<h3 id="timing">Why the heartbeat matters</h3>
	<ul>
		<li>A claim lasts 15 minutes without a heartbeat by default. A heartbeat extends it, but never past 4 hours from when it was made.</li>
		<li>A session with no heartbeat for 3 minutes shows as stale, and after 10 minutes it is abandoned and its claims released. Those are the defaults.</li>
		<li>Every response carries <code>pending</code> counts. When they say something is waiting, the agent calls <code>get_instructions</code>, which hands each instruction over once, and answers with <code>report_back</code>.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>Claims never block anybody. They exist so that the second agent to arrive finds out before it starts.</p>
	<h2 id="claim-narrowly">Claim files narrowly</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Claim the specific files the agent is about to edit, or the narrowest folder that holds them. Paths are relative to the git root.</li>
		<li>A repo-wide or top-level pattern, such as <code>**</code> or <code>app/**</code>, is accepted but recorded at low severity, and it warns nobody. It protects nothing.</li>
		<li><code>*</code> does not cross <code>/</code>: <code>*.go</code> means Go files at the repository root only.</li>
		<li>An absolute path is refused. One declaration takes at most 32 paths.</li>
		<li>Mode <code>read</code> is for files it only reads, and two reads never conflict. Mode <code>structural</code> is for renames, moves and deletes.</li>
		<li>Vendored, built and generated paths are dropped from claims by the project&#39;s ignore patterns.</li>
		<li><code>check_paths</code> asks who else is in some files without claiming anything.</li>
	</ul>
	<h2 id="subagents">Delegating to subagents</h2>
	<p>
		Subagents share their supervisor&#39;s connection and token, so metiche sees them as the same agent. A subagent
		only uses metiche if its brief tells it to. Each one that calls <code>start_session</code> gets its own session,
		and so its own card on the board.
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>The supervisor starts its own session first and puts its <code>session_key</code> in each brief.</li>
		<li>
			Each subagent passes that key as <code>parent_session_key</code>. Its card then shows under the supervisor&#39;s,
			and the runs link to each other. The parent has to be a live or stale session of the same person on the same
			team; any other key is refused with <code>not_found</code>.
		</li>
		<li>Give each subagent its own files. Two subagents that claim one file conflict like any two agents.</li>
		<li>One agent may hold at most 8 open sessions on a team, the supervisor&#39;s included. See <a href="/docs/troubleshooting#session-cap">the session cap</a>.</li>
	</ul>
	<h2 id="asks-you">When an agent asks you</h2>
	<p>Most of the time you hear from metiche only through your agent. It should come to you when:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>a repository is not on the team yet (<a href="/docs/repos-and-teams#the-question">the one-time question</a>);</li>
		<li>you are on several teams and no <code>.metiche</code> file says which one this repository is for;</li>
		<li>it collided with another agent and the two of them could not settle it, for example because both have to rewrite the same code;</li>
		<li>you asked to see the board: it gives you a one-time sign-in link;</li>
		<li>you asked for an invite: it gives you the code, once.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		<code>revoke_invite</code> and <code>sign_out_browsers</code> are marked destructive, and their descriptions tell the
		agent to use them only when you asked.
	</p>
	<div class="docs-note warn">
		<span class="h">Nothing secret goes in a field</span>
		<p>
			Goals, status lines, summaries, notes and paths are shown to the whole team and kept in the team&#39;s event
			log. Tokens, join codes, passwords and customer data never belong in them.
		</p>
	</div>
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- privacy

templ docPrivacy() {
	<p class="docs-lede">
		A board carries file paths, branches, goals and status lines, so it is private unless somebody decided
		otherwise.
	</p>
	<h2 id="private-boards">Private boards are a 404 for outsiders</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Teams are private by default.</li>
		<li>
			A private team&#39;s board is shown only to a browser signed in as a live member of that team. To everyone else,
			including someone signed in who is not a member, it is the same 404 page as a team that does not exist, so a
			board address does not even confirm that the team exists.
		</li>
		<li>Membership is checked on every request and never cached. An open board re-checks about once a minute and stops when your access ends.</li>
		<li>The public list of teams shows only demo recordings. Signed in, it adds your own teams.</li>
		<li>The board holds no credential of its own, and it refuses to start if it is given one. Private boards are read with each signed-in viewer&#39;s own session.</li>
	</ul>
	<h2 id="sign-in-links">One-time, 10-minute sign-in links</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>A link is made by one of your agents with its own token (<code>open_board</code>). It signs one browser in, works once, and expires after 10 minutes.</li>
		<li>An account can hold at most 5 unused links at a time, and make 30 an hour.</li>
		<li>
			The secret is in the part of the address after <code>#</code>, which browsers never send to a server. The sign-in
			page takes it out of the address bar before it makes any request, then sends it in a request body.
		</li>
		<li>A link can only lead to the front page, your list of teams, or one team&#39;s board.</li>
		<li>Treat a link like a password: never paste it into a repository, an issue, a chat or a command line.</li>
	</ul>
	<h2 id="cookies">Cookies</h2>
	<p>
		The board sets one cookie, <code>__Host-metiche_session</code>, and only when you sign in. It is <code>Secure</code>,
		<code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>SameSite=Lax</code> and scoped to this host. It holds your browser session and nothing
		more, and lasts at most 30 days, or 7 days without use. Signing in again replaces the old cookie and ends the old session.
		The docs and the front page set no cookie.
	</p>
	<p>Every form that changes something, such as signing out, revoking a browser or creating an invite, checks that it came from this site and carries a per-session token.</p>
	<h2 id="stored">What is stored, and what never is</h2>
	<div class="docs-table">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th>Secret</th><th>Stored as</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>An agent&#39;s token (<code>mtk_…</code>)</td><td>Its sha256 hash only. The token is shown once, to the client that joined, and cannot be recovered.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>A sign-in link&#39;s secret</td><td>Its sha256 hash only.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>A browser session&#39;s secret</td><td>Its sha256 hash only. The secret itself lives only in your cookie.</td></tr>
				<tr><td>A join code</td><td>As issued, because joining looks an invite up by its code. It is shown only when it is created, never listed, and never written to the team&#39;s event log.</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p>
		For each signed-in browser metiche keeps a short user-agent string and your address cut down to a network prefix
		(IPv4 /24, IPv6 /48). Used or expired sign-in links are deleted a day after they expire. Ended browser sessions are
		deleted a week after they end, or two weeks after they were last used.
	</p>
	<p>
		What agents report (goals, status lines, summaries, paths and notes) is team-visible and kept in the team&#39;s event
		log. When a settled conflict quotes an agent&#39;s note, anything shaped like a credential is masked first.
	</p>
	<h2 id="door-codes">Invites are door codes</h2>
	<p>
		Anyone holding a live join code can join the team and see its board. That is why an invite admits one person and
		lasts seven days by default, why its code is shown once, and why you can revoke it at any time. People who already
		joined keep their access. Share a code privately, never in a repository or a public channel.
		See <a href="/docs/inviting-teammates">Inviting teammates</a>.
	</p>
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- troubleshooting

templ docTroubleshooting() {
	<p class="docs-lede">The problems people actually hit, and what fixes them.</p>
	<h2 id="not-connected">An assistant shows metiche as failed or not connected</h2>
	<ol class="docs-steps">
		<li><b>Restart the assistant.</b> A running assistant does not pick up the configuration the installer just wrote. Quit it completely and open it again.</li>
		<li>
			<b>Re-run the installer.</b> It is safe to run again: it checks the saved token and gives each assistant its own
			verified token. If one assistant fails, nothing is written for that one and the others are kept.
			@docCode("", docInstall)
		</li>
	</ol>
	<p>
		If calls fail with <code>that metiche token is not valid</code>, the token is no longer accepted. The installer
		notices that and starts a new identity, which needs a join code or a new team. If calls fail in a way that looks
		like authentication, the agent can call <code>whoami</code>: it says which agent the token names and which headers
		arrived, and it works without a token. If a call fails with a transport error, <code>health</code> tells whether
		metiche itself is down.
	</p>
	<h2 id="board-404">The board is a 404</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><b>You are not signed in.</b> A private team&#39;s board is a 404 until you sign in as a member. Choose <b>Sign in</b>, or ask your assistant to open the metiche board.</li>
		<li><b>You are signed in, but not as a member of that team.</b> That is the same 404. Ask a teammate for an invite.</li>
		<li><b>Your session ended</b>: you signed out elsewhere, it was revoked, or it expired. The page is a 404 and the cookie is cleared. Sign in again.</li>
		<li><b>The slug is wrong.</b> An unknown team is the same page again, on purpose.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>A page that says the board is unavailable is a different thing: metiche could not be reached. Try again shortly.</p>
	<h2 id="token-refused">"refusing to configure anything with a token that does not authenticate"</h2>
	<p>
		The installer stops with this when <code>METICHE_TOKEN</code> in your environment holds a token the server rejects,
		and that token is neither the one saved in <code>~/.metiche/env</code> nor an old saved one. It treats a token you
		passed yourself as deliberate, so it refuses instead of starting a new identity, and writes nothing.
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>If you did not mean to pass it, open a new terminal without it, or unset <code>METICHE_TOKEN</code>, and run the installer again.</li>
		<li>If you meant a different token, set the right one.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>A rejected <em>saved</em> token is handled for you: the installer says so and starts a new identity.</p>
	<h2 id="stale-token">A stale METICHE_TOKEN in the same shell</h2>
	<p>
		When an install starts a new identity, it rewrites <code>~/.metiche/env</code> and keeps the old file as a backup.
		The terminal it ran in still has the old <code>METICHE_TOKEN</code>. The installer recognises that value from the
		backup and uses the current saved token instead. It warns you here, and again at the end of the run.
	</p>
	<p><b>Open a new terminal.</b> Or load the saved token into this one:</p>
	@docCode("", docEnvReload)
	<h2 id="session-cap">The 8-live-sessions cap</h2>
	<p>A <code>start_session</code> fails with:</p>
	@docCode("", docSessionCap)
	<p>
		One agent may hold at most 8 open sessions (live or stale) on a team. Every terminal and every subagent that started
		a session counts. The message lists them.
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>End the ones that are finished with <code>end_session</code> and their session keys, then start again.</li>
		<li>Make sure each subagent calls <code>end_session</code> when it is done.</li>
		<li>A session that stopped heartbeating is abandoned after 10 minutes by default, and then no longer counts.</li>
	</ul>
	<h2 id="confirm-repo-binding">confirm_repo_binding</h2>
	<p>
		This is not an error, and nothing was created. The repository is not a project on the team yet, and putting it
		there is your decision. Your agent should ask you whether work in the repository goes on the team&#39;s board. Say
		yes, and it starts again with your confirmation and writes a <code>.metiche</code> file. Say no, and it leaves
		metiche out of that repository. See <a href="/docs/repos-and-teams#the-question">Repos and teams</a>.
	</p>
	<p>
		If an agent passes the <code>.metiche</code> confirmation without naming the team, <code>start_session</code> refuses
		and asks for the team as <code>team_slug</code>. And if you are on several teams, a call without a team is refused
		with the list of your team slugs.
	</p>
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- tools

templ docTools() {
	<p class="docs-lede">
		Every tool the metiche MCP server offers today, grouped. Your agent reads the full schemas from the server; this
		is the map. Tools marked <b>read-only</b> change nothing, and the two marked <b>destructive</b> are only for when you ask.
	</p>
	<section id="registered">
		<h2 id="teams">Teams</h2>
		<dl class="tool-list">
			@toolRow("create_team", "", "Create a team and join it in the same call. Returns the team's first join code.")
			@toolRow("join_team", "", "Join a team with a join code, or attach another of your clients to a team you are already on, and get this agent's token.")
			@toolRow("list_teams", "read-only", "Which teams am I on? The one call that works before you have a team or a session.")
		</dl>
		<h2 id="invites">Invites</h2>
		<dl class="tool-list">
			@toolRow("create_invite", "", "Make a join code for a teammate. The code is in this response and nowhere else.")
			@toolRow("list_invites", "read-only", "List the team's invites with their uses, expiry and state, never their codes.")
			@toolRow("revoke_invite", "destructive", "Revoke an invite by its invite_id, so nobody new can join with its code.")
		</dl>
		<h2 id="sessions">Sessions</h2>
		<dl class="tool-list">
			@toolRow("start_session", "", "Begin one piece of work in one repository and get a session_key. A subagent passes its supervisor's key as parent_session_key.")
			@toolRow("heartbeat", "", "Stay alive, extend claims, set the status line and pick up pending counts. About every 60 seconds.")
			@toolRow("end_session", "", "Close a session and release its claims at once.")
		</dl>
		<h2 id="claims">Claims</h2>
		<dl class="tool-list">
			@toolRow("declare_intent", "", "Say what you are about to do and which files you are taking. Conflicts come back in the same response.")
			@toolRow("update_intent", "", "Change the status line, add or drop paths, or mark the intent done.")
			@toolRow("check_paths", "read-only", "Ask who else is in some files without claiming anything.")
		</dl>
		<h2 id="instructions">Instructions</h2>
		<dl class="tool-list">
			@toolRow("get_instructions", "", "Collect what is waiting, such as conflict notices. Reading marks each one delivered, so it is handed over once.")
			@toolRow("report_back", "", "Say what you did about an instruction: done, acknowledged, refused, blocked or not_applicable, with a note.")
		</dl>
		<h2 id="board">The board</h2>
		<dl class="tool-list">
			@toolRow("open_board", "", "Get a sign-in link for the person: one browser, once, 10 minutes. Also returns the plain board address.")
			@toolRow("sign_out_browsers", "destructive", "List the browsers signed in to the account, or sign one or all of them out when asked.")
		</dl>
		<h2 id="state">Identity, team state and health</h2>
		<dl class="tool-list">
			@toolRow("whoami", "read-only", "Which agent does this token name, and which headers reached the server? Works without a token, and says so.")
			@toolRow("get_team_state", "read-only", "See who else is working, on what branch, toward what goal. Scoped and paginated.")
			@toolRow("health", "read-only", "Check whether metiche and its database are up, to tell an outage from a broken session.")
		</dl>
	</section>
	<section id="coming-next">
		<h2>
			@soon()
			Not built yet
		</h2>
		<p>
			These are planned and not on the server today. No tool for them exists, and an agent should skip any step that
			asks for one.
		</p>
		<ul class="soon-list">
			<li><b>Contracts</b>Agents publish the endpoints they produce and consume, and metiche flags a consumer with no producer, or two sides that disagree on a shape.</li>
			<li><b>Decisions</b>The team records decisions, and metiche hands an agent the ones its paths touch, so its own model can judge whether its plan breaks one.</li>
			<li><b>Duplicate work</b>metiche notices two intents aimed at the same issue and tells both agents before either finishes.</li>
			<li><b>Dismissals</b>An agent dismisses a finding as a false positive, and a rule dismissed too often demotes itself to record-only.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>
}

// toolRow is one registered tool. id="tool-<name>" is what docs_test.go
// compares against the tools the server registers.
templ toolRow(name, hint, text string) {
	<div>
		<dt id={ "tool-" + name }>
			<code>{ name }</code>
			if hint != "" {
				<span class="hint">{ hint }</span>
			}
		</dt>
		<dd>{ text }</dd>
	</div>
}
